WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:05.200
Emergence is to me one of the most exciting
things that we can study in science.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:11.959
When patterns spontaneously form in systems
comprised of objects whether they're atoms

00:00:11.959 --> 00:00:19.200
or molecules or nanoparticles or flocks of
birds, those patterns are said to be emergent

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:27.340
because they're not evident by simply looking
at the individual constituents of the system.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:32.800
For example, when you look up in the sky and
see these intricate patterns that birds form

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.500
when they're flying around, those patterns
are nothing that you would have guessed at

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:43.560
if you just looked at the birds and thought
about how the birds are flying next to one

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:44.560
another.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:50.030
If you take water and you freeze it into ice
in the freezer or you make a solution of nanoparticles

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:55.000
and out comes a complicated photonic structure,
you would never have guessed just by looking

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.720
at individual water molecules or individual
nanoparticles what those structures would look like.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:07.880
Emergence underlies so many different types
of questions in science — from the kinds

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:14.560
of traffic jams that you get from the interactions
of many cars on the roads, to even the origin

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:20.800
of life and how complex structures emerged
from simpler structures.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:26.310
In my group, we study emergence by making
mathematical models of different kinds of

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:33.360
systems, and then we solve these models using
computers, usually very large, very fast computers,

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:41.110
and what's important about using the computer
is that we're able to strip away one-by-one,

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:45.750
all the kinds of interactions that might be
present in a system so that we can see the

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:49.000
role of all of the different interactions.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:55.180
One of the contributions to emergence that
my students and I are particularly fascinated

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.800
by is the idea of entropy.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.119
Entropy is typically thought of as disorder.

00:02:02.119 --> 00:02:06.909
The idea that entropy can actually contribute
to order is sometime surprising to people,

00:02:06.909 --> 00:02:15.410
but in fact entropy can be the cause of emergence
in many different systems.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:17.340
That is something that's really interesting
to us.

